0 = Empty              7 = Mostly Real (PROD BASELINE)
3 = Styled Demo        8 = Production-Ready
5 = Wired Frontend     9 = Hardened
6 = Half-Real          10 = Enterprise-Grade

Card 6: Test Severity (P0-P4)

P0 = Blocker  → 24h fix → Security vuln, core feature broken
P1 = Critical → 1 week  → Major feature broken, workaround exists
P2 = High     → 2 weeks → Minor feature issue, edge case
P3 = Medium   → When possible → Cosmetic issue
P4 = Low      → Optional → Documentation typo

Card 7: Build Order Priority

1. Replace mocks first (anything fake blocks maturity)
2. Missing core features (user-requested functionality)
3. Error handling and resilience (boundaries, loading, retry)
4. Accessibility (keyboard, ARIA, screen reader)
5. Performance (lazy loading, code splitting, caching)
6. Analytics and monitoring (visibility into production)
7. Polish (animations, micro-interactions, refinement)

Card 8: Stop Conditions

1. Maturity score >= target (default: 7)
2. User explicitly says ship it
3. Remaining gaps are polish-only (score >= 8)
4. After 5 full build cycles (hard limit)

Card 9: Leverage Score Formula

Leverage = (UserImpact x FeatureUnlocks x PainReduction)
           / (Effort x Risk x Dependencies)

> 5.0  = Build immediately
3-5.0  = Build soon
1.5-3  = Build later
< 1.5  = Skip or icebox

Card 10: Backend Critical Rules

1. Never hand-write TS interfaces for DB → use $inferSelect
2. Never write raw SQL → use Drizzle API
3. Never modify api/lib/ or api/kimi/ → framework internals
4. Never use 'call' as router name → use 'phone'
5. Never use serial() for FK → use bigint with mode
6. Never drop tables to fix schema → use db:push
7. Never modify .env values → pre-configured by init

